questions.question:
Consider the following recursive function:
int fun(int n) {
  static int x = 1;
  if (n == 1) return x;
  n = n + 1;
  x = x + 1;
  return fun(n - 1);
}
What value is returned by fun(5)?

Consider the C code for computing tree height recursively:
int height(Node *root) {
  if (root == NULL) return 0;
  int h1 = height(root->left);
  int h2 = height(root->right);
  return 1 + (h1 > h2 ? h1 : h2);
}
If a tree has 1 node, what will it return?

For the function:
void get(int n) {
  if (n < 1) return;
  get(n - 1);
  get(n - 3);
  printf("%d ", n);
}
How many total calls to get() are made when get(4) is invoked?

What does the following recursive function print?
void reversePrint(char *str) {
  if (*str) {
    reversePrint(str + 1);
    putchar(*str);
  }
}
For string input 'GATE'?

What is the base condition in Euclid's algorithm for finding GCD recursively:
int gcd(int a, int b) {
  if (b == 0) return a;
  return gcd(b, a % b);
}

